Good sharpening of the question. Your architecture has specific properties that most simulation frameworks don't: parent agents spawn children, children report back to parents, parents make decisions based on aggregated child results, and every agent at every level stores its own state independently. That's a tree that grows and prunes itself. Let me think about what genuinely needs that structure rather than just being a flat swarm.
Recursive market research. A top-level agent gets "analyse the UK restaurant industry." It reasons about how to decompose that and spawns children: one for fast food, one for fine dining, one for casual dining, one for delivery-only. Each of those reasons about its sub-sector and spawns further children: the fast food agent spawns one for burger chains, one for chicken, one for pizza, one for coffee shops. Each leaf agent does actual research (web scraping, data gathering) and writes a focused micro-report that it stores independently. Then the results flow back up — the fast food parent reads its children's reports and synthesises a fast food overview, the top-level agent reads all the sector overviews and produces the final analysis. The key thing your architecture gives you here is that every intermediate node stores its own synthesis. You don't just get a final report — you get a navigable tree of reports at every level of granularity. The user can read the top-level summary or drill down into "what's happening specifically in UK pizza delivery in the Midlands." And the decomposition itself isn't scripted — the parent agents decide how to split the problem based on what they learn.
Organisational simulation. Model a company as a hierarchy — CEO agent spawns VP agents, VPs spawn director agents, directors spawn team-lead agents, team-leads spawn individual contributor agents. Each agent has a budget, goals, and constraints inherited from their parent. The CEO sets "reduce costs by 15%," each VP figures out how to apply that to their division and passes targets down, each layer interprets and adapts the directive for their context. The leaf agents (individual contributors) reason about how the mandate affects their actual work: "my team lead wants 20% fewer cloud resources but my service needs to handle Black Friday traffic." They report problems back up. The interesting emergent behaviour is how information gets distorted as it flows down (the "telephone game" of corporate communication) and how problems get filtered as they flow back up (middle management hiding bad news). Each agent stores its own interpretation of the directive and its own response, so you can audit the full chain afterward and see exactly where communication broke down. Run it with different organisational structures — flat vs deep hierarchy, wide vs narrow spans of control — and compare how effectively directives propagate. Management consultants would find this genuinely useful.
Fractal geography modelling. Top agent: "model the ecological health of the Amazon basin." It spawns agents for major regions (western Amazon, eastern Amazon, river corridors, deforestation frontier). Each region agent spawns sub-region agents. Each sub-region spawns agents for individual ecological zones. The leaf agents model their local hectare — rainfall, canopy cover, species diversity, soil health. But here's where the hierarchy matters: effects flow both up and down. A leaf agent reports "my canopy is gone, soil erosion increasing." Its parent aggregates: "40% of my sub-region has lost canopy, this is now affecting regional rainfall patterns." The parent then pushes updated rainfall data back down to all its children, including the ones that still have canopy: "your rainfall is dropping because your neighbours lost their trees." The parent-child communication creates feedback loops that a flat model can't express as naturally. Each level stores its own state — the regional agents store regional summaries, the leaf agents store local conditions — and you can query any level independently.
Legislation impact analysis. Start with a proposed law — say, "ban on single-use plastics." A top-level agent spawns industry-sector agents: food packaging, retail, hospitality, medical, logistics. Each sector agent reasons about first-order impacts and spawns children for sub-impacts: the food packaging agent spawns one for "cost of alternative materials," one for "supply chain disruption," one for "consumer behaviour change," one for "small business vs large business impact." Those children can spawn further — the supply chain agent might spawn agents for specific materials (paper, bioplastic, glass, aluminium) to research availability and pricing. Results flow back up with each parent synthesising its children's findings into a sector-level impact assessment. The top-level agent then reasons across sectors to find cross-cutting themes and conflicts: "the hospitality sector's switch to paper increases demand, but the logistics sector's analysis shows paper supply is already constrained." The tree structure means you naturally get both depth (detailed material-by-material analysis) and breadth (cross-sector synthesis), and every node is independently queryable. Run it for 50 different proposed laws and you've built something policy analysts would actually use.
Debugging your own system — but hierarchically. A root agent monitors the overall health of a website build pipeline. It spawns children for each subsystem: Kafka health, Postgres health, K8s scheduling, LLM inference, Cloudflare deployment. Each of those spawns children for specific components: the Kafka agent spawns one per broker, plus one for topic lag, plus one for consumer group balance. The Postgres agent spawns one for connection pool metrics, one for slow queries, one for disk usage, one for replication lag. Each leaf agent monitors its specific metric and stores its own findings independently. But the hierarchy adds causal reasoning at each level. The Kafka parent doesn't just aggregate "broker 2 is slow" — it reasons about why: "broker 2 is slow AND topic creation is backed up AND the partition count is high, therefore the controller is overloaded." It reports that synthesis to the root agent, which correlates across subsystems: "Kafka controller overload is causing agent spawn delays, which is causing workflow timeouts, which is creating retry storms that make the Kafka problem worse." The tree structure mirrors the actual dependency structure of your system. And because each agent stores its findings independently, you get a full diagnostic tree you can navigate after the fact — not just an alert, but an explanation.
Scientific literature mapping. Give the root agent a research question: "what are the current approaches to treating antibiotic resistance?" It spawns children for major approaches: phage therapy, new antibiotic development, combination therapies, non-antibiotic approaches, prevention strategies. Each child researches its area and spawns further children for specific sub-topics — the phage therapy agent spawns agents for "engineering phages," "phage cocktails," "regulatory status," "clinical trials." Leaf agents find and summarise specific papers or data points. Results flow up with each parent synthesising the landscape in its sub-area. The top-level agent produces a structured literature review that's actually a navigable tree. The fractal structure means the depth of investigation is adaptive — if an area turns out to be rich and complex, its agent spawns more children; if an area is thin, it stays as a leaf. Each stored result is a standalone micro-review that's useful on its own. Scale this to a million agents and you could map an entire scientific field — not just summarise it, but structure it into a queryable knowledge hierarchy.
Supply chain stress testing. Model a global supply chain as a tree. Top agent: "a major manufacturer." It spawns tier-1 supplier agents, each of which spawns tier-2 suppliers, and so on down to raw materials. Each agent knows its lead times, inventory, alternative sources, and financial health. Introduce a disruption at any level — "the factory in Shenzhen that makes this specific chip just flooded" — and watch the effects propagate up the tree. Each agent reasons about its response: can I find an alternative supplier? How long can I run on existing inventory? Do I need to warn my parent? The parent agents aggregate the damage: "3 of my 5 suppliers are affected, I can keep producing at 60% capacity for 2 weeks, then I'm stuck." This flows all the way up to the top-level agent making strategic decisions. The stored state at each node means you can replay the disruption afterward and see exactly which node made which decision and when. Run the same disruption 100 times with slightly different agent reasoning temperatures and you get a probability distribution of supply chain resilience.
Evolutionary idea generation. Start with a root agent that has a problem statement. It spawns 10 child agents, each of which proposes a different solution. The root evaluates the solutions, picks the 3 best, and tells each winner to "reproduce" — each winning agent spawns 3-4 children that are variations on its approach (mutations). The next generation gets evaluated, winners reproduce, losers are pruned. This is genetic algorithm logic but with LLM reasoning at each node — the "mutations" aren't random, they're reasoned variations ("what if I took my parent's approach but used a different data structure?" or "what if I kept the core idea but applied it to a different market?"). The tree structure is literally an evolutionary tree, and every node stores its proposed solution independently. After 20 generations, you can trace the lineage of the best solution back through its ancestors and see how the idea evolved. At 1M agents across many generations, you'd have a massive solution space explored with reasoning, not randomness.
The common thread in all of these is that the hierarchy isn't just organisational convenience — it's semantically meaningful. Parent-child relationships represent real decomposition (a sector into sub-sectors, a region into sub-regions, a problem into sub-problems), and the flow of information up and down the tree represents real aggregation and delegation. That's what makes your architecture a better fit for these than a flat message-passing swarm.

