The interesting thing about your system isn't just "a million processes running" — anyone can spin up a million containers doing curl. What you have is a million reasoning entities that can make decisions, spawn children, and coordinate through message passing. That's a different thing entirely. Let me think about what genuinely exploits that.
Simulating an economy. Give each agent a persona — a business, a consumer, a regulator — with starting capital, preferences, and simple rules. Let them trade with each other through Kafka messages. Businesses set prices, consumers shop around, some agents are suppliers, some are middlemen. You don't script the outcomes — you let a million LLM-powered actors make self-interested decisions and watch what emerges. Does price equilibrium form? Do monopolies emerge? Do agents find arbitrage opportunities the designer didn't anticipate? Economists simulate this with rule-based agents, but nobody's done it with a million agents that can actually reason about their choices in natural language. The output is a dataset that economists would find genuinely interesting.
Collaborative knowledge mapping. Give each agent a single Wikipedia article (there are about 6.7 million English articles, so 1M is a decent chunk). Each agent reads its article, extracts the key claims, and then messages neighbouring agents (articles that link to each other) to cross-reference and fact-check. "You say the population of Lagos is 15 million, but my article on Nigeria says 21 million — which of us is outdated?" The agents negotiate disagreements and produce a cleaned, cross-referenced knowledge graph. The output is a million-node graph with annotated edges showing where Wikipedia contradicts itself. That's a publishable dataset.
A million code reviewers. Point them at a large open-source codebase — the Linux kernel has about 30 million lines across thousands of files. Each agent gets a file or a function, analyses it for bugs, security issues, style problems, and dead code, then messages agents responsible for related files to check cross-file dependencies. "Your function calls my function with a null pointer that I never validate — one of us has a bug." The hierarchical spawning means a coordinator agent can assign subsystems, spawn reviewers, and aggregate findings. The output is a structured audit of a major codebase done by a swarm. Even if the individual findings are mediocre (7B models aren't great at deep code analysis), the sheer coverage and cross-referencing is something no human team could replicate at that scale.
Emergent language. Start 1M agents with no shared language. Each one can send messages to a small number of neighbours (like a graph network). Give them a coordination task — say, they need to agree on a common set of labels for 100 objects, but they start with no vocabulary. They can only communicate by sending short strings to their neighbours and seeing what responses come back. Over thousands of message rounds, do they converge on a shared vocabulary? How many distinct "languages" form? Do dialects emerge at network boundaries? This is a genuine research question in computational linguistics and emergent communication. It's been done with small numbers of neural agents in labs, but never at this scale with LLM-powered agents.
Distributed creative writing. Not one novel — a million interconnected micro-stories. Each agent writes a 200-word vignette about a character in a fictional city. But each agent knows about its character's three closest neighbours (the agents adjacent in the network), and their stories must be consistent. If agent A writes that there was a fire on Main Street, agents B and C (who also live on Main Street) need to incorporate that. The agents negotiate plot points through messages. The output is a million interlocking stories that form a coherent (or interestingly incoherent) tapestry of a fictional world. You could render it as a navigable map — click on any block and read that character's story, with links to the neighbours.
Adversarial red-teaming at scale. Give half the agents the job of defending a system (a simulated network, a set of policies, a logical argument) and the other half the job of attacking it. The attackers spawn sub-agents to probe different angles, the defenders spawn sub-agents to patch vulnerabilities. This is basically war-gaming with LLM reasoning. The interesting output isn't who wins — it's the strategies that emerge from the escalation. What attack patterns do the agents invent? What defensive formations do they converge on?
The one I think is most impressive to demo though is the economy simulation. Here's why: it produces emergent behaviour that nobody designed, it's visually compelling (you can render the trading network, price movements, wealth distribution in real-time), it touches on something people care about (economics, inequality, market dynamics), and it's genuinely novel — nobody has run a million-agent LLM-powered economy. The compute is modest per agent (each "turn" is a short reasoning step — "given my inventory and these prices, what should I buy/sell?"), so a 7B model on GPU handles it fast. And the output is a dataset and visualisation, not a million websites that nobody will visit.
The website demo proves the infrastructure works. The economy simulation proves the infrastructure thinks.

