Open-source LLMs on CPU — what actually works
The honest truth about CPU inference is that it's slow. A 7B parameter model running on a modern server CPU (say, a 32-core Xeon) with quantised weights (Q4_K_M via llama.cpp) generates roughly 10-20 tokens/second. That's workable for your use case because your agents don't need real-time chat — they generate content, wait for it, and move on. A 1,500 token response takes 75-150 seconds on CPU. Slower than an API call (which returns in 5-15 seconds), but if you're running thousands of agents in parallel and the bottleneck is throughput not latency, it's fine — you just need enough inference servers.
The models worth considering:
For content generation (the bulk of your agents): Mistral 7B, Llama 3.1 8B, or Qwen 2.5 7B. These are the sweet spot for CPU inference — small enough to run on commodity hardware, capable enough to write marketing copy, plan site architecture, and do the kind of structured reasoning your workflows need. They're noticeably worse than Claude Sonnet at nuanced writing, but for generating hero section copy or about pages to a template, they're adequate.
For the smarter tasks (strategy, architecture planning): Mixtral 8x7B or Llama 3.1 70B. These need more resources. Mixtral runs on CPU but slowly (it's a mixture-of-experts model, so active parameters are lower than the headline number). The 70B models really want GPU or a very large memory server (40-50GB RAM just for the model weights quantised to Q4). This is where your "sparingly use GPU" budget goes.
For the research agents: These do web scraping analysis and summarisation. The 7-8B models handle this well — it's mostly extraction and reformulation, not creative work.
Inference infrastructure
For CPU inference at scale, you'd run vLLM or llama.cpp server (or the more production-ready text-generation-inference from HuggingFace) behind a load balancer. Each server hosts one model and serves an OpenAI-compatible API, so your chassis code barely changes — you just point the LLM adapter at a different URL.
A single 32-core server running a 7B model can handle roughly 5-10 concurrent requests (depending on context length and batching). For 10K agents with staggered execution, you'd peak at maybe 300-500 concurrent agents, each needing one LLM call at a time. That's 30-100 inference servers for CPU-only.
That sounds like a lot, but these don't need to be dedicated machines. If you're on Rackspace bare metal, a single high-core-count server (64 cores, 128GB RAM) could run 2-3 model instances. For the stress test, you'd burst these for hours, not months.
For GPU inference, one decent GPU (A100, or even a consumer A6000) running a 7B model with vLLM can handle 50-200 concurrent requests with continuous batching. Two or three GPUs could replace 50+ CPU servers for the 7B workload. If Rackspace offers GPU instances (they do have some), this is significantly more cost-effective per-request.
The 1M agent run — a practical plan
Here's where it gets interesting. You want it to be impressive, use real agent intelligence, but not bankrupt you. Let me sketch something.
The task: Generate 1 million unique micro-sites. Not full 6-page websites — that would be 6M+ content pieces and the LLM cost even on self-hosted would be enormous in compute time. Instead, each agent gets a randomly generated business profile (name, industry, location, one-line description) and produces a single-page site with a hero section, three feature bullets, and a call to action. One LLM call per agent. One page deployed to Cloudflare. One million distinct businesses, one million live URLs.
This is genuinely impressive as a demonstration — a million live websites created autonomously — and it exercises every part of your system: Kafka messaging, workflow orchestration, LLM inference, content generation, template rendering, and deployment. But it keeps the per-agent cost minimal.
LLM budget for 1M agents, one call each:
On CPU with a 7B model, the cost is just electricity and server rental. No per-token charges. The question is how long it takes and how many servers you need.
If each agent needs one LLM call producing ~500 tokens (a compact single-page site), at 15 tokens/second on CPU, that's about 33 seconds of inference per agent. With 1M agents, that's 33M seconds of inference time — roughly 9,200 hours of single-server compute.
If you want to complete the run in 24 hours, you need ~380 CPU inference servers running continuously. In 48 hours, ~190 servers. In a week, ~55 servers.
With GPU, the math changes dramatically. A single A100 running vLLM with a 7B model and continuous batching can process maybe 1,000-2,000 requests per minute (short outputs). That's 60-120K requests per hour. To do 1M in 24 hours, you'd need roughly 8-17 GPUs. To do it in 48 hours, 4-9 GPUs.
Cost estimates for the 1M run at Rackspace:
ApproachServers needed (48hr run)Estimated costCPU only (bare metal, 64-core)~60-100 servers$3,000-6,000 (spot/burst pricing for 2 days)GPU only (A100 instances)5-10 GPUs$500-2,000 (at ~$2-4/hr per GPU for 48hrs)Hybrid (GPU for inference, CPU for everything else)5-8 GPUs + 10-20 CPU nodes for K8s/Kafka/DB$1,000-3,000
The hybrid approach makes the most sense. Your K8s worker pools, Kafka brokers, and Postgres run on CPU nodes. The LLM inference runs on a small GPU cluster. The agents themselves are lightweight goroutines in worker pools (at 1M scale, you've moved past the one-Pod-per-agent model).
The other infrastructure for 1M:

Kafka: Shared topic pools, 10-20 brokers, maybe 3-5 dedicated nodes. You need this for the message throughput — 1M agents × a few messages each = several million messages over 48 hours. Kafka handles this easily, the broker count is more about partition count and consumer group management.
Postgres / CockroachDB: 1M rows in orchestration_states. Postgres can handle this on a single beefy node, but you'd want CockroachDB or Citus for the write throughput if agents are completing at 5-10K per hour (that's 1-3 writes/second per agent, peaking at thousands of concurrent writes).
Cloudflare deployment: If you actually deploy 1M sites, Cloudflare Pages has a free tier but you'd hit API rate limits. Cloudflare Workers with KV store could serve 1M micro-sites from a single Worker script with KV lookups. That would be cheap — Workers free tier is 100K requests/day, paid is $5/month for 10M requests. The KV writes are $5 per million — so $5 to write 1M sites.

The phased plan
Phase 1 (now-ish): Stub LLM stress test at 10K agents. Costs near zero. Finds your Kafka/K8s/Postgres ceilings.
Phase 2: Set up llama.cpp or vLLM serving a 7B model on one or two machines. Modify your LLM adapter to point at the local endpoint. Run 100-500 real agents against it. Validate content quality is acceptable for the use case. This costs you the server rental for a few days — maybe $50-100.
Phase 3: Implement the worker pool model (replace one-Job-per-agent with long-running workers pulling from shared Kafka topics). Test at 10K agents with local LLM. This is the biggest code change and the one that unlocks everything above 10K.
Phase 4: Implement multi-cluster dispatch. Set up 2-3 clusters (could even be 2-3 Rackspace regions, or just separate K8s clusters on the same hardware). Run at 50-100K agents.
Phase 5: The million-agent run. Rent the GPU cluster for a weekend, pre-provision the Kafka and DB infrastructure, run it, record everything, tear it down. Budget $2,000-5,000 all-in for a 48-hour burst, depending on how much you can get from Rackspace at burst pricing.
The headline when you're done: "1 million autonomous AI agents, 1 million unique websites, 48 hours, self-hosted models, zero API costs." That's a genuinely impressive thing to have pulled off, and it demonstrates every piece of the architecture at real scale.
The thing that would take the most calendar time isn't the infrastructure — it's Phase 3, the worker pool refactor. That's the architectural shift that makes everything above 10K feasible without drowning in K8s Job overhead. Everything else is configuration and provisioning.

